{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nrebar has three detached-child spawn sites that are near-duplicates of one another:\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/push.py` — the async branch of `push_tickets_branch`\n- `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` — `_spawn_detached_drain` (+ `_detach_kwargs`)\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact_trigger.py` — `_spawn_detached_sweep` (+ `_detach_kwargs`)\n\nEach independently repeats: the `parents[2]` PYTHONPATH bootstrap so a bare-python child can\nimport rebar, the `-c` stub that re-inserts `src` and calls back into rebar, the platform\ndetach flags, the stdio discipline, and the never-raise wrapper. The duplication is not\naccidental and it is documented in the code itself — `compact_trigger._detach_kwargs` says\n\"mirroring :func:`rebar.llm.enrich_drain._detach_kwargs`\", `_spawn_detached_sweep` says\n\"Mirrors ``enrich_drain._spawn_detached_drain``\", and `enrich_drain._spawn_detached_drain`\nsays \"Mirrors push.py's PYTHONPATH bootstrap\".\n\n## Why this matters — a defect has already propagated along this seam\n\nBug `3198-438c-72a5-470f` was a missing `cwd=` on the detached `Popen`: the child inherited\nthe spawning command's working directory, and when that was a `make worktree` worktree since\nremoved, the child died at `os.getcwd()`. It was present at ALL THREE sites, and it got there\nby deliberate imitation across three separate tickets:\n\n- `57114212cf` (2026-06-12, pre-trailer) introduced the pattern in `push.py`;\n- `c1de-d6a0-6cef-4135` copied it into the drain — its plan says outright \"POSIX reuses\n  push.py's approach (`start_new_session=True`, `close_fds=True`) + its PYTHONPATH bootstrap\";\n- `1fde-becd-942a-4351` copied it again into the compaction sweep.\n\nOne omission, replicated three times over two months, because \"spawn a detached rebar child\"\nhas no single implementation to get right once. The next property this pattern needs — a\nsignal handler, an env projection, a resource limit, an fd discipline — will propagate the\nsame way.\n\n## Scope (to be confirmed by whoever claims this)\n- A single `spawn_detached(...)` in `src/rebar/_proc.py` (already the documented single source\n  of truth for logic shared between detached spawn sites, and already home to\n  `reap_process_group` and `detached_child_cwd`; it is a deliberate stdlib-only leaf with no\n  `rebar.*` imports, which must be preserved). It would own the PYTHONPATH bootstrap, the\n  detach flags and the durable cwd, and it RE-RAISES on failure — the never-raise posture\n  stays per-caller, because the three sites do not catch identically (push.py catches only\n  `OSError`, logging `%r`; drain/sweep catch broad `Exception` with `exc_info=True`) and\n  folding the catch into the helper would silently broaden push.py's exposure. Each caller\n  keeps its existing `except` clause and its own log line, so the refactor changes no\n  exception surface. Callers would supply the child\n  entry point, their stderr sink, and their child ENV — env construction stays per-caller\n  because it differs materially (push.py passes its secret-stripped\n  `project_child_env(relationship=\"unrelated\")` projection plus `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`,\n  the drain/sweep pass a plain `{**os.environ}`); the helper only layers the PYTHONPATH\n  bootstrap onto whatever env the caller provides, so consolidation cannot drop\n  push.py's secret-stripping.\n- The three call sites above collapse onto it.\n- Related open question, deliberately NOT changed while fixing 3198: should\n  `_config_sources.repo_root`'s final `Path.cwd()` fallback DEGRADE rather than raise? It was\n  left raising on purpose — a silently-wrong repo root is worse than a loud failure, and the\n  three detached children no longer reach it now that they get an explicit cwd. But the\n  exposure survives for long-lived IN-PROCESS work, which cannot be given its own cwd: daemon\n  threads at `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:575`, `src/rebar/llm/workflow/editor.py:523`,\n  `src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py:440`,\n  `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_heartbeat.py:69`, and a reconciler that\n  `src/rebar/config.py:516` says \"can run as a daemon from anywhere\". Decide that posture\n  once, here, rather than per-site.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Exactly one implementation of \"spawn a detached rebar child\" exists; the three sites\n      call it and none re-implements the bootstrap or the detach flags.\n- [ ] A STATIC test (source scan over `src/rebar`) enforces that the signature of a\n      DETACHED-REBAR-CHILD spawn appears only in `_proc.py`: the Windows detach tokens\n      (`DETACHED_PROCESS` / `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`) and the bare-python re-entry bootstrap\n      (the `-c` stub doing `sys.path.insert(0, sys.argv[...])` back into `rebar.*`).\n      A fourth un-exercised copy-paste site then fails the test rather than slipping\n      past a runtime-only assertion. The three NON-detached `start_new_session` spawn\n      sites (`grounding/harness.py`, `adapters/jira/acli_subprocess.py`,\n      `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` — process-group reaping, not rebar\n      re-entry) are out of scope and must stay untouched.\n- [ ] `_proc.py` remains a stdlib-only leaf with no `rebar.*` imports.\n- [ ] A recorded decision (accept or change) on `repo_root`'s cwd fallback for long-lived\n      in-process callers — recorded as a comment on this ticket AND in the docstring at\n      `_config_sources.repo_root` (verified by inspecting that location); if the decision is\n      'change', the degraded fallback is implemented.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and CI's pytest invocation are green.\n\n## Provenance\nObserved while fixing `3198-438c-72a5-470f`. The cwd omission itself is fixed at all three\nsites; this is the structural reason it was three sites and not one."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787338760200484001,"uuid":"a46dd95e-e1e6-46a5-a67d-bf1187e064c0"}